        "id": "ch6_3_4-1",
        "text": "Applets\nA surprise feature of the Xfce panel is the ability to add applets. You wouldn\u2019t\nexpect such a feature from a bare-bones graphical desktop environment, but Xfce\nprovides quite a few fancy applets that you can add to the panel. Follow these\nsteps to add a new applet:\n1. Right-click an empty spot on the panel.\nA pop-up menu appears, allowing you to move applet icons, remove them, or\nadd new ones.\n2. Hover over the Panel menu option.\n3. Select Add New Items from the menu.\nThe Add New Items dialog box appears, showing a list of the applets available\nto add, as shown in Figure 6-12. From here you can also add an Application\nLauncher icon, or add a separator to produce a blank space between applet\nicons in the panel.\nCHAPTER 6 Exploring Alternative Desktops 117\n4. Find the applet you want to add, and then click the Add button.\n5. Right-click the app icon in the panel, and then select Move.\n6. Move the applet icon to the location on the panel where you\u2019d like it to\nstay.\nBefore long you can have your Xfce desktop customized to look and behave just\nlike the fancier GNOME 3 or KDE Plasma desktop environments!\nFIGURE 6-12:\nThe Xfce Add New\nItems dialog box.",
